Method 1: Temporary Alias (Current Session Only) On Windows 

To create an alias that only lasts until you close the terminal,
This method allows you to use "tf plan" or "tf apply" instead of "terraform plan"


RUN:
Set-Alias tf terraform

Test the alias:

tf --version


-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


Method 2: Using PowerShell Profile (Recommended)


1. Open PowerShell and type the following to open your profile in Notepad

RUN:

if (!(Test-Path -Path $PROFILE)) { New-Item -ItemType File -Path $PROFILE -Force }
notepad $PROFILE



2. Add the alias function to the notepad file

function tf { terraform $args }


3. Save and close Notepad.

4. Reload your profile or restart PowerShell

. $PROFILE


5. Test the alias

tf --version

-------------------------------------------------------------

Note:
When PowerShell starts:
It automatically runs $PROFILE
But execution policy = Restricted (default)
So it blocks your alias function



To fix any error: like  running scripts is disabled on this system


* Open PowerShell run as administrator 

Run this command:
Set-ExecutionPolicy RemoteSigned   ( effect all user ) 
type: Y

//or//

Run this for Alternative (You can allow only for your user)
Set-ExecutionPolicy -Scope CurrentUser RemoteSigned
type: Y

Then Close and reopen PowerShell (normal mode is fine now)


What it does ?
RemoteSigned =
Local scripts (like your profile) ✅ allowed
Downloaded scripts ⚠️ need signature
👉 This is safe + commonly used by DevOps engineers

